  "solution": {
    "text": "Given&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br /><br />Pair of straight lines passing through the origin.<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8757;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp; or&nbsp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br /><br />Normal exists at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;which is on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br /><br /><math> <mrow> <mo>&#8658;</mo> </mrow> </math>&nbsp; The slope of normal at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;<br /><br /><math> <mrow> <mo>&#8756;</mo> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;&nbsp;Equation of normal will be<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfenced></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math><br /><br />Now, find the point of intersection with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.&nbsp;<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></math><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;----------------<br />&nbsp;&nbsp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=-1,&#160;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=3</mo></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8757;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> lies in the fourth quadrant.",
